1
00:01:14,942 --> 00:01:17,377
Yes, I've got it on my desk.

2
00:01:17,811 --> 00:01:19,780
Well, the bank branches
are well-located,

3
00:01:19,947 --> 00:01:23,213
they're firmly established,
but its management,

4
00:01:23,383 --> 00:01:25,443
el stinko.

5
00:01:25,619 --> 00:01:27,419
So are you in favor of
the acquisition or not?

6
00:01:27,554 --> 00:01:28,886
Well, I've drawn up a contract

7
00:01:29,056 --> 00:01:31,355
wherein the Hayworth Fund
will purchase Crestmore Bank

8
00:01:31,525 --> 00:01:32,857
contingent upon the removal

9
00:01:33,026 --> 00:01:35,996
of all middle and upper
management personnel.

10
00:01:36,163 --> 00:01:38,689
Ooh. Tough stance.

11
00:01:38,865 --> 00:01:40,800
But sounds like a prudent one.

12
00:01:40,968 --> 00:01:43,369
Let me look over the contracts
before you formalize anything,

13
00:01:43,537 --> 00:01:45,199
- if you don't mind.
- Of course.

14
00:01:45,372 --> 00:01:49,104
Moving on. David and
the Remlinger case.

15
00:01:49,276 --> 00:01:52,735
Still taking depos, looking to
go to trial on November 22nd.

16
00:01:52,913 --> 00:01:54,473
- There goes your Thanksgiving.
- Mm-hm.

17
00:01:55,449 --> 00:01:59,352
That brings us to Harriet and
the status of the Ship case.

18
00:02:02,155 --> 00:02:03,487
The Ship case?

19
00:02:04,191 --> 00:02:06,558
Yeah. I told you
about it last week.

20
00:02:06,727 --> 00:02:09,196
You said you'd be glad
to take it on, remember?

21
00:02:10,864 --> 00:02:13,857
No. I'm sorry.

22
00:02:15,736 --> 00:02:18,467
Send in the Ship file, please.

23
00:02:18,639 --> 00:02:19,839
Harriet, I'm surprised at you.

24
00:02:20,007 --> 00:02:23,808
I thought you realized how
important this case is to us.

25
00:02:25,045 --> 00:02:29,540
- That's "ship" as in "partnership."
- Ha, ha, ha.

26
00:02:29,716 --> 00:02:31,810
- Congratulations, Harriet.
- Congratulations.

27
00:02:31,985 --> 00:02:34,420
And let me just say that when
it comes to beating the odds,

28
00:02:34,588 --> 00:02:36,113
Harriet here takes the cake.

29
00:02:36,289 --> 00:02:38,053
The carrot cake, to be exact.

30
00:02:38,225 --> 00:02:39,454
Nice going, lady.

31
00:02:39,626 --> 00:02:42,221
She started out as a
paralegal, went back to school,

32
00:02:42,396 --> 00:02:46,731
worked odd jobs and
reared a child all by herself.

33
00:02:47,167 --> 00:02:48,795
Harriet,

34
00:02:49,336 --> 00:02:52,465
you have a great attitude
and a terrific legal instinct.

35
00:02:52,639 --> 00:02:53,698
But, the truth be told,

36
00:02:53,874 --> 00:02:56,810
there's only one reason we
decided to make you a partner.

37
00:02:57,310 --> 00:03:00,280
And that's so that none of us
would ever have to face you in court.

38
00:03:01,748 --> 00:03:03,649
- Congratulations.
- Here's to you.

39
00:03:03,817 --> 00:03:05,877
- Well said.
- Congratulations, Harriet.

40
00:03:06,053 --> 00:03:07,453
Way to go.

41
00:03:08,488 --> 00:03:10,150
This looks good.

42
00:03:10,323 --> 00:03:13,452
- Nice work, Lisa. Very nice.
- Thank you.

43
00:03:13,627 --> 00:03:15,721
You haven't forgotten our
dinner with Taki Yamamoto,

44
00:03:15,896 --> 00:03:17,262
ComParks Corporation, tonight?

45
00:03:17,431 --> 00:03:19,024
Seven o'clock, The Terrace.

46
00:03:19,199 --> 00:03:21,464
Prying their U.S. legal work
away from Meldrick & Taylor

47
00:03:21,635 --> 00:03:23,001
would be quite a coup.

48
00:03:23,170 --> 00:03:24,729
Heh. I'll bring my crowbar.

49
00:03:24,905 --> 00:03:28,501
Oh, and Lisa, remember that dress
you wore last month to the banquet?

50
00:03:28,675 --> 00:03:30,644
- The red one?
- Yeah?

51
00:03:30,977 --> 00:03:32,001
Wear it tonight.

52
00:03:33,847 --> 00:03:35,008
Yeah?

53
00:03:35,182 --> 00:03:36,582
Put him through.

54
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:39,310
Nelson, talk to me.

55
00:03:44,391 --> 00:03:52,391
Fifty-one, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56.

56
00:03:54,334 --> 00:03:55,700
Yeah.

57
00:04:01,608 --> 00:04:03,406
Ben?

58
00:04:04,978 --> 00:04:07,106
Ben, you here?

59
00:04:14,454 --> 00:04:16,047
Damn it, Billy.

60
00:04:16,223 --> 00:04:18,920
Don't mess with the TV.

61
00:04:19,092 --> 00:04:21,425
When you turn it off,
it goes back to one.

62
00:04:21,595 --> 00:04:24,497
- I'm sorry.
- Well, don't mess with it.

63
00:04:24,664 --> 00:04:28,294
- What channel did you want?
- Fifty-six. <i>The Godfather's</i> on.

64
00:04:28,468 --> 00:04:31,529
Well, just punch in 56.

65
00:04:34,674 --> 00:04:37,439
Don't mess with the TV.

66
00:04:37,744 --> 00:04:40,009
Works on mine.

67
00:04:40,547 --> 00:04:43,039
Course it's state-of-the-art.

68
00:04:43,216 --> 00:04:46,186
Bought it last week. Paid cash.

69
00:04:47,621 --> 00:04:49,453
Guess what else I just bought?

70
00:04:49,623 --> 00:04:51,524
The Brooklyn Bridge?

71
00:04:51,691 --> 00:04:53,091
The house next door.

72
00:04:53,527 --> 00:04:55,928
- What house next door?
- To you.

73
00:04:56,096 --> 00:04:59,863
I made an offer today on that
house right over there. Heh, heh.

74
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:03,095
We're gonna be neighbors, Ben.

75
00:05:03,436 --> 00:05:06,804
Oh, no. No.

76
00:05:06,973 --> 00:05:09,238
Oh, no, not that house.

77
00:05:09,409 --> 00:05:10,604
Why not?

78
00:05:10,777 --> 00:05:14,214
Oh, it's... It's jinxed.

79
00:05:14,948 --> 00:05:16,541
- Jinxed?
- Yes.

80
00:05:16,716 --> 00:05:18,548
A lot of people died over there.

81
00:05:18,952 --> 00:05:20,682
- How?
- Oh, I don't know.

82
00:05:20,854 --> 00:05:24,154
They died all kinds of
ways. A lot of them died.

83
00:05:24,324 --> 00:05:26,520
You need to get your offer back.

84
00:05:26,893 --> 00:05:28,333
- No.
- Well, you know... You know...

85
00:05:28,495 --> 00:05:30,054
You know what would be better?

86
00:05:31,865 --> 00:05:36,860
Take that money and get
yourself a nice lady friend.

87
00:05:37,170 --> 00:05:40,607
Spend that money on her, buy
her some candy, some flowers,

88
00:05:40,774 --> 00:05:43,039
take her to the movies.

89
00:05:43,210 --> 00:05:45,236
You could use a
nice lady friend.

90
00:05:45,745 --> 00:05:47,475
No, Ben.

91
00:05:47,647 --> 00:05:52,551
At my age, it's just one
long buildup, and then "pffft."

92
00:05:53,019 --> 00:05:58,287
No, sir, I can't let this
opportunity pass me by.

93
00:05:58,458 --> 00:06:01,053
Why, you know, heh,

94
00:06:02,195 --> 00:06:08,567
we could be sitting here watching
TV together every night. Heh.

95
00:06:11,371 --> 00:06:12,464
Got any salt?

96
00:06:21,848 --> 00:06:25,285
But in order to build such an
amusement park here in your city,

97
00:06:25,452 --> 00:06:28,183
my company needs
local financing.

98
00:06:28,355 --> 00:06:29,789
Well, there's the Hayworth Fund.

99
00:06:31,191 --> 00:06:34,320
We wish to apply to the fund
for construction completion loans.

100
00:06:34,494 --> 00:06:37,828
We are prepared to pay your
client double the current lending rate.

101
00:06:39,499 --> 00:06:41,764
You'll find all the
specifics in here.

102
00:06:43,270 --> 00:06:45,637
Thank you, Taki. Actually,
Lisa's in charge of the fund.

103
00:06:48,541 --> 00:06:51,841
Well, I'll review your
proposal tonight...

104
00:06:53,046 --> 00:06:54,406
and I'll get back
to you tomorrow.

105
00:06:54,547 --> 00:06:55,810
Excellent.

106
00:06:55,982 --> 00:06:57,974
We would like to break
ground immediately.

107
00:06:58,718 --> 00:07:00,518
You know, Taki, I'm
aware of your relationship

108
00:07:00,654 --> 00:07:02,350
with Meldrick &
Taylor in New York,

109
00:07:03,189 --> 00:07:04,919
but retaining
local legal counsel

110
00:07:05,091 --> 00:07:06,889
is something you would
do well to consider.

111
00:07:07,060 --> 00:07:09,461
- Think it would expedite matters?
- I think it would indeed.

112
00:07:09,996 --> 00:07:12,124
Then I shall indeed consider.

113
00:07:12,465 --> 00:07:15,697
Well, if you two will excuse
me, I have to get home.

114
00:07:16,403 --> 00:07:20,704
My daughter calls from
college every week 9:00 sharp.

115
00:07:21,875 --> 00:07:25,277
Lisa, I'm sure Mr. Yamamoto would
appreciate a ride back to his hotel.

116
00:07:25,445 --> 00:07:27,175
Of course.

117
00:07:28,248 --> 00:07:30,376
- Thank you for a pleasant evening.
- Thank you.

118
00:07:30,550 --> 00:07:34,009
And I will talk to you
tomorrow, Taki. Good night.

119
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:50,365
- You're a very good driver.
- Thank you.

120
00:07:50,537 --> 00:07:52,028
Not that I'm surprised.

121
00:07:52,205 --> 00:07:54,401
Bruce tells me you're
good at many things.

122
00:07:57,477 --> 00:08:00,072
I prefer to drink champagne
at this time of night.

123
00:08:00,246 --> 00:08:02,306
Is that all right with you?

124
00:08:02,816 --> 00:08:03,840
Excuse me?

125
00:08:04,017 --> 00:08:06,350
I can call the hotel and
have a bottle waiting.

126
00:08:06,519 --> 00:08:08,249
Or would you prefer to
drink something else?

127
00:08:08,421 --> 00:08:12,188
Oh, that's... I appreciate that,
but I'm just gonna drop you off.

128
00:08:12,359 --> 00:08:14,555
I've gotta get home. I
still have some work to do.

129
00:08:15,428 --> 00:08:18,592
But Bruce indicated that you
would be coming up to my room.

130
00:08:20,333 --> 00:08:21,858
He did?

131
00:08:22,035 --> 00:08:24,300
He said it was just one
of the many services

132
00:08:24,471 --> 00:08:27,373
the law firm of Dixon and Parmelee
would be happy to provide me

133
00:08:27,540 --> 00:08:30,203
now and in the future.

134
00:08:37,584 --> 00:08:38,882
He said what?

135
00:08:40,653 --> 00:08:42,246
Perhaps I misunderstood.

136
00:08:43,890 --> 00:08:46,792
Yes, I'm afraid you did.

137
00:09:07,714 --> 00:09:11,617
<i>- Yes?</i>
- It's Lisa Swift. I need to talk to you.

138
00:09:31,771 --> 00:09:32,795
What's wrong?

139
00:09:32,972 --> 00:09:35,441
I just dropped
Mr. Yamamoto off at his hotel.

140
00:09:35,608 --> 00:09:37,509
- And?
- Hello, Lisa.

141
00:09:38,178 --> 00:09:40,647
- Hello, Natalie.
- Are you all right?

142
00:09:40,814 --> 00:09:42,476
Yeah, I'm fine.

143
00:09:42,649 --> 00:09:44,447
Can we do this in private?

144
00:09:44,617 --> 00:09:47,746
Sure. Let's go in my
study. It's this way.

145
00:10:00,767 --> 00:10:03,396
Mr. Yamamoto wanted
me to go to bed with him.

146
00:10:04,170 --> 00:10:06,036
He expected me to.

147
00:10:06,206 --> 00:10:10,473
In fact, he said that you
said that it was part of my job.

148
00:10:10,844 --> 00:10:12,904
What did you do?

149
00:10:13,379 --> 00:10:14,847
What do you think I did?

150
00:10:16,649 --> 00:10:20,882
Adding a big successful Japanese
firm like ComParks to our client roster

151
00:10:21,054 --> 00:10:23,546
could open a hell of
a lot of doors, Lisa.

152
00:10:24,290 --> 00:10:25,314
What are you saying?

153
00:10:26,259 --> 00:10:27,591
You know what I'm saying.

154
00:10:28,461 --> 00:10:30,657
No. No, I don't get this at all.

155
00:10:30,830 --> 00:10:33,766
First you molest me at dinner,
and then you pimp me out?

156
00:10:34,801 --> 00:10:36,030
I didn't molest you.

157
00:10:36,202 --> 00:10:38,228
Your hand was all over my leg.

158
00:10:40,373 --> 00:10:42,706
My God, Lisa.

159
00:10:43,076 --> 00:10:44,806
What's the matter with you?

160
00:10:44,978 --> 00:10:51,214
I gave you a brief, momentary
pat out of friendship and pride.

161
00:10:52,619 --> 00:10:54,713
No, that is not what it
was, and you know it.

162
00:10:54,888 --> 00:10:56,448
Well, you have your
depiction of events,

163
00:10:56,589 --> 00:10:57,989
- and I have mine.
- Yeah.

164
00:10:58,158 --> 00:10:59,990
Are you no longer happy
at Dixon and Parmelee?

165
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:01,253
Because if you aren't...

166
00:11:01,528 --> 00:11:03,520
Oh, now, what is that,
some kind of threat?

167
00:11:03,696 --> 00:11:05,927
No, it's not a threat.

168
00:11:06,099 --> 00:11:08,432
I just asked you a question.

169
00:11:09,235 --> 00:11:11,033
Maybe you're just tired.

170
00:11:11,204 --> 00:11:13,230
Why don't you go
home, think things over,

171
00:11:13,406 --> 00:11:14,704
and we'll talk tomorrow.

172
00:11:34,460 --> 00:11:37,221
Here's the tentative contract I drew
up on behalf of the Hayworth Fund

173
00:11:37,363 --> 00:11:38,797
for that ComParks loan.

174
00:11:38,965 --> 00:11:40,331
Good.

175
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:43,026
I managed to smooth
things out with Taki.

176
00:11:43,203 --> 00:11:45,138
I'm having lunch with
him this afternoon.

177
00:11:45,305 --> 00:11:47,297
I'll show it to him then.

178
00:11:49,008 --> 00:11:50,943
I'm, um...

179
00:11:51,411 --> 00:11:53,141
I'm sorry about what
happened last night.

180
00:11:53,313 --> 00:11:54,993
I would like it if we
could clean the slate

181
00:11:55,148 --> 00:11:57,117
and go on as if
nothing ever happened.

182
00:11:57,951 --> 00:11:59,146
Done.

183
00:12:00,153 --> 00:12:01,678
Good.

184
00:12:01,955 --> 00:12:05,722
Lisa, I want you to come
by my house tonight at 9:00.

185
00:12:06,326 --> 00:12:07,350
What for?

186
00:12:08,461 --> 00:12:11,056
So that I can debrief
you on my lunch with Taki.

187
00:12:11,231 --> 00:12:12,961
I won't have time
to do it before then.

188
00:12:15,668 --> 00:12:20,231
Bruce, I'm not gonna
get involved with you.

189
00:12:20,873 --> 00:12:24,901
I love working here, and I don't mind
wearing my red dress occasionally,

190
00:12:25,078 --> 00:12:28,048
but that is as far as it's gonna
go. I can tell you that right now.

191
00:12:29,515 --> 00:12:32,713
My house, tonight, 9:00.

192
00:12:56,709 --> 00:12:59,543
<i>- Who is it?</i>
- It's Lisa.

193
00:13:32,845 --> 00:13:34,905
Bruce.

194
00:13:49,896 --> 00:13:51,831
Bruce?

195
00:13:55,802 --> 00:13:57,828
Natalie?

196
00:14:09,148 --> 00:14:10,810
Bruce?

197
00:15:11,277 --> 00:15:12,711
Ahem. Miss Swift?

198
00:15:14,781 --> 00:15:16,682
Mr. Matlock.

199
00:15:16,849 --> 00:15:17,942
Thank you for coming.

200
00:15:21,454 --> 00:15:23,014
Give me a yell when
you're ready to go.

201
00:15:23,122 --> 00:15:25,114
Okay, Francis.

202
00:15:26,392 --> 00:15:29,453
I have to be honest here.

203
00:15:29,629 --> 00:15:31,257
Bruce Parmelee
was a friend of mine

204
00:15:31,431 --> 00:15:35,869
and I only came down
here as, I guess, a curiosity.

205
00:15:36,035 --> 00:15:38,470
That's fine. But if you
were hoping to get a look

206
00:15:38,638 --> 00:15:40,607
at the person who
killed him, it wasn't me.

207
00:15:41,441 --> 00:15:43,171
They say his wife saw you.

208
00:15:43,709 --> 00:15:46,144
Standing over him,
yes, but not stabbing him.

209
00:15:46,312 --> 00:15:47,905
She couldn't have,
he was already dead.

210
00:15:48,748 --> 00:15:50,876
It must've just happened.

211
00:15:51,050 --> 00:15:52,610
I know that he was
alive when I drove up

212
00:15:52,752 --> 00:15:55,244
because he talked to me when
he let me in through the gate.

213
00:15:55,421 --> 00:15:57,481
There were no fingerprints
on the murder weapon.

214
00:15:57,657 --> 00:15:59,922
It was a letter opener
with a carved handle.

215
00:16:00,092 --> 00:16:02,254
The surface was too
uneven to hold prints.

216
00:16:03,162 --> 00:16:05,529
And, as far as I know, there
was nobody else in the house.

217
00:16:05,698 --> 00:16:08,759
They don't have a live-in
maid and his kids are in college.

218
00:16:08,935 --> 00:16:11,268
His wife was at some
play or something.

219
00:16:11,437 --> 00:16:14,601
She parked behind my car, so I
know she wasn't there when I drove up.

220
00:16:15,842 --> 00:16:19,870
- You'd make a good courtroom lawyer.
- Oh. Heh.

221
00:16:20,379 --> 00:16:22,041
I know.

222
00:16:22,248 --> 00:16:24,683
- Bruce was your boss?
- Yes.

223
00:16:25,184 --> 00:16:26,208
You get along?

224
00:16:27,487 --> 00:16:29,319
Yes.

225
00:16:31,190 --> 00:16:34,957
Well, without a motive, the
prosecution doesn't have much of a case.

226
00:16:37,230 --> 00:16:40,632
Except Bruce was harassing me.

227
00:16:41,133 --> 00:16:44,126
He was the ideal boss until
just a couple of days ago.

228
00:16:44,303 --> 00:16:47,637
Out of the blue, he touched
me quite inappropriately,

229
00:16:47,807 --> 00:16:49,967
and told a potential client
that I would sleep with him

230
00:16:50,109 --> 00:16:52,078
as a reward for
signing with the firm.

231
00:16:52,245 --> 00:16:53,713
Bruce Parmelee?

232
00:16:54,080 --> 00:16:56,549
Just yesterday he
propositioned me for himself.

233
00:16:56,716 --> 00:16:59,652
I told him I was not interested,
I would never be interested,

234
00:17:00,953 --> 00:17:05,448
and he insisted, demanded, that
I show up at his house last night.

235
00:17:05,625 --> 00:17:07,719
I don't know why, but I went.

236
00:17:08,294 --> 00:17:11,059
Bruce Parmelee.

237
00:17:12,265 --> 00:17:15,326
Well, uh, did...?

238
00:17:15,501 --> 00:17:17,436
Did anything, uh...?

239
00:17:17,737 --> 00:17:18,761
Definitely not.

240
00:17:18,938 --> 00:17:20,065
Did you fight?

241
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:23,669
- He was already dead when I got there.
- Oh.

242
00:17:23,843 --> 00:17:27,473
Did any of your co-workers
hear or see any of this?

243
00:17:27,647 --> 00:17:29,115
Bruce was too smart.

244
00:17:29,282 --> 00:17:31,808
It was all very subtle.

245
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:36,989
I haven't told the police
about this for obvious reasons.

246
00:17:37,990 --> 00:17:39,618
But it's the truth.

247
00:17:43,496 --> 00:17:45,761
Bruce Parmelee.

248
00:17:53,439 --> 00:17:56,500
I had meetings with
him, had lunches with him,

249
00:17:56,676 --> 00:17:59,805
and I saw him all the
time at the courthouse.

250
00:17:59,979 --> 00:18:02,574
- You think she was lying?
- I don't know.

251
00:18:02,748 --> 00:18:06,617
- You think she's telling the truth?
- I don't know.

252
00:18:08,754 --> 00:18:11,849
But I knew Bruce Parmelee.

253
00:18:12,291 --> 00:18:16,194
Well, if he was harassing her,
you can bet that there were others.

254
00:18:16,362 --> 00:18:18,763
Maybe one of them killed him.

255
00:18:19,231 --> 00:18:20,255
Ben.

256
00:18:20,433 --> 00:18:22,095
Ben, I just came
from my realtor.

257
00:18:22,268 --> 00:18:24,828
Someone else made an
offer on that house next door.

258
00:18:25,004 --> 00:18:26,028
Really?

259
00:18:26,205 --> 00:18:30,142
My realtor said I should give it to
them, but I want us to be neighbors, Ben.

260
00:18:30,309 --> 00:18:31,675
I'm gonna up my offer.

261
00:18:31,844 --> 00:18:35,906
Sure, I think you should go
for it if you like it. Life's too short.

262
00:18:36,082 --> 00:18:37,516
You wanna stay out of it?

263
00:18:37,683 --> 00:18:39,743
Wait, wait, this
could be a trick.

264
00:18:39,919 --> 00:18:41,839
The seller could've found
out how loaded you are,

265
00:18:41,988 --> 00:18:44,583
and made the offer themself
just to jack up the price.

266
00:18:45,291 --> 00:18:46,884
Oh.

267
00:18:47,493 --> 00:18:49,394
I'd really hate that.

268
00:18:49,562 --> 00:18:51,292
Oh, sure you would.

269
00:18:51,464 --> 00:18:53,433
Nobody wants to be
taken advantage of.

270
00:18:53,599 --> 00:18:55,864
I'd hate it myself.

271
00:18:56,035 --> 00:18:59,528
So just go on home and
let that other sucker have it.

272
00:18:59,705 --> 00:19:02,300
Don't think about it anymore.

273
00:19:02,942 --> 00:19:06,435
Put a little more
thought into getting a girl.

274
00:19:13,653 --> 00:19:16,589
My friend felt ill and
left at intermission,

275
00:19:16,756 --> 00:19:20,659
so rather than sit through the
whole second act all by myself,

276
00:19:20,826 --> 00:19:22,727
I went home too.

277
00:19:23,295 --> 00:19:28,427
Well, did Bruce tell you that
Lisa was coming over that night?

278
00:19:28,601 --> 00:19:30,297
No.

279
00:19:30,803 --> 00:19:37,368
Was he expecting anybody
else to come over that night?

280
00:19:37,543 --> 00:19:40,877
Well, he was still at work
when I left for the play,

281
00:19:41,047 --> 00:19:44,609
- so I really couldn't say.
- Uh-huh.

282
00:19:45,785 --> 00:19:52,419
Did coworkers, like Lisa, come
over after hours very often?

283
00:19:52,792 --> 00:19:54,488
Not often, no.

284
00:19:54,660 --> 00:19:57,459
But they did whenever
it was necessary.

285
00:19:58,597 --> 00:20:01,431
Did he ever talk
to you about work?

286
00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:04,468
Oh, yes. Yes, he made quite
a point of keeping me informed

287
00:20:04,637 --> 00:20:07,801
with what was going on,
so I wouldn't feel left out.

288
00:20:07,973 --> 00:20:09,703
He was a very considerate man.

289
00:20:10,443 --> 00:20:13,936
Did you ever get
the feeling that he

290
00:20:14,113 --> 00:20:16,947
you know, wasn't
telling you everything?

291
00:20:24,557 --> 00:20:29,120
Bruce gave Amy, David
and me whatever we needed,

292
00:20:29,295 --> 00:20:30,786
as soon as we needed it.

293
00:20:31,097 --> 00:20:35,296
He was a good husband
and a wonderful father.

294
00:20:35,468 --> 00:20:39,200
And he never for a moment
gave me reason to complain.

295
00:20:40,406 --> 00:20:42,602
Now, I have things to do.

296
00:20:42,975 --> 00:20:45,376
- Okay.
- Excuse me.

297
00:21:00,493 --> 00:21:02,428
Michael?

298
00:21:15,741 --> 00:21:18,074
- Michael?
- Yeah?

299
00:21:18,244 --> 00:21:21,373
Come in here right now.

300
00:21:27,186 --> 00:21:29,746
You were supposed
to start supper.

301
00:21:29,922 --> 00:21:32,756
I'm sorry. I fell asleep.

302
00:21:32,925 --> 00:21:34,723
You fell asleep

303
00:21:35,494 --> 00:21:38,464
because you stayed up
half the night watching TV.

304
00:21:38,631 --> 00:21:42,693
Look, Michael, I work my
tail off at the office all day long.

305
00:21:42,868 --> 00:21:44,666
I come home, and
what do I get to do?

306
00:21:44,837 --> 00:21:46,863
Start all over again
working my tail off.

307
00:21:47,039 --> 00:21:48,667
I'm sorry, Mom. Okay?

308
00:21:50,876 --> 00:21:54,142
If it's one of your friends,
tell them you're busy.

309
00:22:00,519 --> 00:22:02,920
- He wants to talk to you.
- Thank you. Thank you.

310
00:22:03,088 --> 00:22:04,989
I'm Cliff Lewis. I
work with Ben Matlock.

311
00:22:05,157 --> 00:22:06,955
- We're representing Lisa Swift.
- Oh, yeah.

312
00:22:07,126 --> 00:22:10,460
Can we talk while I'm fixing
supper? I'm running a little behind.

313
00:22:10,629 --> 00:22:12,530
Sure, whatever you need.

314
00:22:12,698 --> 00:22:14,858
Being an attorney yourself,
you probably have some idea

315
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:16,832
what I need to ask you.

316
00:22:17,002 --> 00:22:18,300
Probably, if he had any enemies.

317
00:22:19,772 --> 00:22:21,741
Michael, turn off that TV.

318
00:22:21,907 --> 00:22:23,273
And the answer is no.

319
00:22:23,442 --> 00:22:25,172
None that I knew of.

320
00:22:25,344 --> 00:22:28,872
But he was a little
upset with Lisa that day.

321
00:22:29,348 --> 00:22:30,407
He was?

322
00:22:30,583 --> 00:22:34,247
Mm. He said that she'd almost
blown our signing ComParks,

323
00:22:34,420 --> 00:22:37,049
that big Japanese
construction company.

324
00:22:37,223 --> 00:22:40,887
I'd had to stay at work until
almost 8:30 because of it.

325
00:22:41,060 --> 00:22:43,427
So I was a little
miffed with her myself.

326
00:22:43,596 --> 00:22:46,436
Did you know that Mr. Parmelee told
her to come to his house that night?

327
00:22:46,565 --> 00:22:50,502
No. But he did say he was
going to have a talk with her, so...

328
00:22:50,669 --> 00:22:51,728
Excuse me.

329
00:22:52,371 --> 00:22:54,533
How did he treat his
female employees?

330
00:22:55,007 --> 00:22:57,499
Like competent human beings.

331
00:22:57,676 --> 00:22:59,668
He never engaged in any behavior

332
00:22:59,845 --> 00:23:02,508
that might be considered
sexual harassment?

333
00:23:02,681 --> 00:23:04,547
No. Never.

334
00:23:05,351 --> 00:23:08,446
So then it would be a
surprise to you if I told you

335
00:23:08,621 --> 00:23:11,284
that he might have
been harassing Lisa?

336
00:23:12,625 --> 00:23:14,253
Bruce Parmelee?

337
00:23:15,628 --> 00:23:19,463
She was shocked. I mean,
shocked. Just like Ben was.

338
00:23:19,632 --> 00:23:22,261
You know, Jerri,
I hate to say it,

339
00:23:22,434 --> 00:23:25,734
but maybe Lisa's lying
about this harassment stuff.

340
00:23:25,905 --> 00:23:28,345
If she wanted to lie, don't you
think she would invent a story

341
00:23:28,474 --> 00:23:30,409
that made her look
innocent instead of guilty?

342
00:23:30,576 --> 00:23:33,444
All right, then maybe
she's just imagining it.

343
00:23:33,913 --> 00:23:35,438
I don't know.

344
00:23:35,614 --> 00:23:38,550
The more I hear about how
absolutely above reproach

345
00:23:38,717 --> 00:23:41,152
this Bruce Parmelee was,
the more convinced I am

346
00:23:41,320 --> 00:23:43,380
that he was doing exactly
what she says he was.

347
00:23:43,989 --> 00:23:45,013
Yeah.

348
00:23:45,190 --> 00:23:47,785
Everyone engages in sexist
behavior at one time or another.

349
00:23:47,960 --> 00:23:48,984
Even women.

350
00:23:49,161 --> 00:23:52,654
But you show me a guy who hasn't
so much as told a dumb-blond joke,

351
00:23:52,831 --> 00:23:55,665
and I'll show you somebody
who's hiding something.

352
00:23:57,169 --> 00:24:00,435
Whoa. He wired nearly
half a million dollars

353
00:24:00,606 --> 00:24:03,235
to a bank in Mayville,
Louisiana last year.

354
00:24:03,509 --> 00:24:05,671
May...? Mayville?

355
00:24:05,844 --> 00:24:07,437
Hold on a second. Hold on.

356
00:24:08,314 --> 00:24:09,373
Mayville, Louisiana.

357
00:24:09,548 --> 00:24:12,143
Remember that woman
who used to work here?

358
00:24:12,318 --> 00:24:13,718
She resigned. Marilyn Reese?

359
00:24:15,054 --> 00:24:16,613
That's where she
moved. Mayville.

360
00:24:16,789 --> 00:24:18,655
Shh. I hear something.

361
00:24:24,763 --> 00:24:27,323
You scared me. I thought
you'd all gone home for the night.

362
00:24:27,499 --> 00:24:30,469
- What are you doing?
- My job.

363
00:24:30,636 --> 00:24:33,504
The janitor's already been here
and gone. Left two hours ago.

364
00:24:33,672 --> 00:24:37,507
I know. I'm quality control.
Would you excuse me, please?

365
00:24:37,676 --> 00:24:41,238
You must be the richest
quality-control man around.

366
00:24:41,413 --> 00:24:42,893
You and I have the
same running shoes.

367
00:24:43,048 --> 00:24:44,710
They cost about
140 bucks a pair.

368
00:24:44,883 --> 00:24:46,351
- Get out of the way.
- Who says?

369
00:24:46,518 --> 00:24:48,680
- Get out of my way!
- Hey, wait a second. Whoa, whoa!

370
00:24:48,854 --> 00:24:50,720
Are you all right?
Are you all right?

371
00:24:50,889 --> 00:24:52,009
- You okay?
- Yeah, I'm fine.

372
00:24:52,157 --> 00:24:54,183
What are you, nuts?
You almost got us killed.

373
00:24:54,360 --> 00:24:58,354
- I wonder what he was after.
- Well, let's just take a look and see.

374
00:24:58,597 --> 00:25:00,930
Hm. Well, unless they
changed the alphabet,

375
00:25:01,100 --> 00:25:04,002
shouldn't ComParks come
before Continental Travel?

376
00:25:04,169 --> 00:25:06,968
This file was put back
in the wrong place.

377
00:25:22,254 --> 00:25:25,053
- What are you doing?
- Shadowboxing.

378
00:25:25,224 --> 00:25:28,956
- You a boxer?
- Yeah. Keeps the edge up.

379
00:25:29,128 --> 00:25:30,687
No, no, no.

380
00:25:30,863 --> 00:25:31,956
Sorry.

381
00:25:32,131 --> 00:25:35,624
We had a little run-in tonight
at Parmelee and Dixon.

382
00:25:36,402 --> 00:25:38,030
You're good.

383
00:25:38,237 --> 00:25:40,433
You had a run-in?
What kind of run-in?

384
00:25:41,707 --> 00:25:43,767
Well, we found this guy
messing around with a file

385
00:25:43,942 --> 00:25:47,037
of what could be one of Bruce
Parmelee's biggest clients.

386
00:25:47,379 --> 00:25:48,847
Cliff's gonna check
it out tomorrow.

387
00:25:49,014 --> 00:25:50,175
What happened to the guy?

388
00:25:51,583 --> 00:25:55,418
I kind of stomped on his foot
with my heel and ground it in a little.

389
00:25:56,922 --> 00:25:58,584
Then what?

390
00:25:59,425 --> 00:26:03,123
He pushed me into Cliff and
we fell down and he got away.

391
00:26:04,463 --> 00:26:06,932
You better watch messing with
somebody bigger than you are.

392
00:26:07,566 --> 00:26:09,166
How do you know he
was bigger than I was?

393
00:26:09,334 --> 00:26:10,893
Just a guess.

394
00:26:11,170 --> 00:26:12,604
Well, he was a little bigger.

395
00:26:12,771 --> 00:26:14,330
You better watch.

396
00:26:14,506 --> 00:26:15,530
Okay.

397
00:26:16,442 --> 00:26:17,808
Want a piece of pie?

398
00:26:18,744 --> 00:26:21,475
- Yeah.
- Come on.

399
00:26:27,619 --> 00:26:29,099
I'm sorry these are
late, Your Honor,

400
00:26:29,254 --> 00:26:31,723
but I had to make some
last-minute adjustments.

401
00:26:31,890 --> 00:26:34,860
The People hereby charge
Lisa Swift with murder.

402
00:26:35,027 --> 00:26:37,155
Wait a minute.

403
00:26:37,329 --> 00:26:39,409
I'm sorry to spring it on
you like this, Mr. Matlock,

404
00:26:39,565 --> 00:26:43,400
but I got this from the police
less than 20 minutes ago.

405
00:26:44,103 --> 00:26:47,369
Now, according to their analysis of
the ribbon in the electric typewriter

406
00:26:47,539 --> 00:26:50,168
that Bruce Parmelee
had in his office,

407
00:26:50,342 --> 00:26:53,141
he typed this evaluation of
the defendant's job performance

408
00:26:53,312 --> 00:26:54,610
the day of his murder.

409
00:26:54,780 --> 00:26:56,408
Anybody could've typed this.

410
00:26:56,582 --> 00:26:58,380
Well, the State is
prepared to prove

411
00:26:58,550 --> 00:27:01,611
that Lisa Swift went to
the victim's home that night

412
00:27:01,787 --> 00:27:03,881
enraged about this letter.

413
00:27:04,056 --> 00:27:05,251
And that was her motive.

414
00:27:05,424 --> 00:27:07,984
Mr. Matlock, would
you like a continuance

415
00:27:08,160 --> 00:27:11,153
so that you could confer with
your client before you enter a plea?

416
00:27:12,965 --> 00:27:16,129
Defendant pleads not guilty.

417
00:27:25,577 --> 00:27:28,741
That letter, you didn't
see it the night you...?

418
00:27:28,914 --> 00:27:30,382
No, no.

419
00:27:30,549 --> 00:27:33,144
So you never saw it and
you never destroyed it?

420
00:27:33,318 --> 00:27:35,398
Two days after the murder,
I received it in the mail.

421
00:27:35,554 --> 00:27:37,874
He must have written it after
I rejected him in his office.

422
00:27:38,023 --> 00:27:39,992
- What did you do with it?
- I destroyed it.

423
00:27:40,159 --> 00:27:41,752
Why didn't you tell me?

424
00:27:41,927 --> 00:27:43,687
Well, why? It wouldn't
have changed anything.

425
00:27:43,829 --> 00:27:44,956
Lisa.

426
00:27:45,130 --> 00:27:47,895
I'm sorry I'm late. How
was the arraignment?

427
00:27:48,667 --> 00:27:50,397
They just charged
me with murder.

428
00:27:51,136 --> 00:27:52,832
What?

429
00:27:53,205 --> 00:27:54,298
Ben Matlock.

430
00:27:54,673 --> 00:27:55,697
- Harriet Dern.
- Hello.

431
00:27:56,108 --> 00:27:58,869
Everyone at the office thought that
if they charged you with anything,

432
00:27:59,011 --> 00:28:00,877
it would be some
kind of manslaughter.

433
00:28:01,046 --> 00:28:02,326
They're saying that I killed him

434
00:28:02,481 --> 00:28:04,416
because I found out that
he was going to fire me.

435
00:28:05,617 --> 00:28:07,313
Why on earth would he do that?

436
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:09,713
Because I wouldn't
go to bed with him.

437
00:28:11,657 --> 00:28:15,458
Harriet, I know what you said
to Mr. Lewis the other night,

438
00:28:15,627 --> 00:28:19,189
but I just cannot believe
that I was the only one.

439
00:28:19,731 --> 00:28:21,393
Bruce never did anything to you?

440
00:28:21,700 --> 00:28:22,895
Oh, no.

441
00:28:23,902 --> 00:28:27,600
He didn't touch you or proposition you
or try to get you to sleep with a client?

442
00:28:27,773 --> 00:28:30,106
Lisa, Bruce wouldn't
do things like that.

443
00:28:30,275 --> 00:28:32,176
Yes, he would.
He did them to me.

444
00:28:32,344 --> 00:28:36,247
- No, don't... Don't...
- Lisa, this won't fly as a defense.

445
00:28:36,415 --> 00:28:39,317
- Why are you saying these things?
- Because it's the truth!

446
00:28:39,484 --> 00:28:41,146
- Don't... No. Let's go.
- Ugh.

447
00:28:41,320 --> 00:28:42,845
Excuse us.

448
00:28:58,604 --> 00:29:01,039
- Hi.
- Hi.

449
00:29:01,306 --> 00:29:03,775
I heard they blindsided
you in the arraignment.

450
00:29:03,942 --> 00:29:06,036
You might say.

451
00:29:08,447 --> 00:29:11,474
I wanted to talk to you
before I went to Louisiana.

452
00:29:11,750 --> 00:29:14,276
You've been a bad boy, Ben.

453
00:29:14,886 --> 00:29:17,219
Or should I say "Waldo"?

454
00:29:18,657 --> 00:29:21,024
- Waldo?
- Waldo Finney?

455
00:29:21,193 --> 00:29:25,096
The name you used when you
made the offer on the house next door.

456
00:29:25,564 --> 00:29:29,194
I got curious and I
did some digging.

457
00:29:29,868 --> 00:29:31,234
Does Billy know?

458
00:29:31,570 --> 00:29:32,799
No.

459
00:29:32,971 --> 00:29:34,735
At least I didn't tell him.

460
00:29:34,906 --> 00:29:36,670
I think it's something
you should do.

461
00:29:38,577 --> 00:29:41,843
Yeah, you're right.

462
00:29:42,581 --> 00:29:44,447
I'll tell him.

463
00:29:46,251 --> 00:29:47,549
As soon as hell freezes over.

464
00:29:49,888 --> 00:29:53,655
You would've done the
same thing and you know it.

465
00:29:56,028 --> 00:29:59,487
True. But I wouldn't
have gotten caught at it.

466
00:30:23,522 --> 00:30:25,286
Mr. Yamamoto?

467
00:30:25,457 --> 00:30:27,323
Hi, I'm Cliff Lewis.

468
00:30:27,492 --> 00:30:28,516
I work with Ben Matlock.

469
00:30:28,694 --> 00:30:32,062
He's representing the woman
accused of killing Bruce Parmelee.

470
00:30:32,230 --> 00:30:34,790
Horrible tragedy. Senseless.

471
00:30:34,966 --> 00:30:37,663
But, unfortunately, such
things are all too common

472
00:30:37,836 --> 00:30:39,270
in this great country of yours.

473
00:30:39,438 --> 00:30:42,602
Yeah. You had some
business with Mr. Parmelee?

474
00:30:42,774 --> 00:30:44,014
He controlled the Hayworth Fund

475
00:30:44,142 --> 00:30:46,304
and signed the loan that
allowed us to break ground.

476
00:30:47,579 --> 00:30:49,070
- That's funny.
- Why's that?

477
00:30:49,247 --> 00:30:52,615
Well, I talked to his partners.
They were against that loan.

478
00:30:52,784 --> 00:30:55,879
They said they convinced him not to
sign it until he cleared up a few things.

479
00:30:56,221 --> 00:30:59,919
But he signed the contract. I
have a copy, so does the bank.

480
00:31:00,092 --> 00:31:02,652
Yeah, well, so does Dixon-Parmelee.
Actually, it was in this file

481
00:31:02,794 --> 00:31:05,889
that the dozer jockey over there
was going through the other night.

482
00:31:06,064 --> 00:31:07,259
You must be mistaken.

483
00:31:07,432 --> 00:31:09,424
Oh, no, no, I don't
think so. I don't think so.

484
00:31:09,601 --> 00:31:13,299
He was in an awfully big
hurry to get out of there.

485
00:31:13,472 --> 00:31:15,304
He planted the
signature page, didn't he?

486
00:31:15,474 --> 00:31:17,306
We would not be
party to such a thing.

487
00:31:18,076 --> 00:31:19,908
A handwriting expert
went over this file.

488
00:31:20,078 --> 00:31:23,446
Everything in it's okay except the
signature on the signature page.

489
00:31:23,615 --> 00:31:24,913
It's a forgery.

490
00:31:25,517 --> 00:31:27,850
Bruce Parmelee decided
not to authorize that loan,

491
00:31:28,019 --> 00:31:29,453
and when he told you that,

492
00:31:29,821 --> 00:31:33,019
well, let's just say, you
found that news unacceptable.

493
00:31:33,191 --> 00:31:35,626
Are you suggesting I had
something to do with his death?

494
00:31:36,661 --> 00:31:38,687
- Well, it was a thought.
- You're wrong.

495
00:31:38,864 --> 00:31:42,528
And as for any and all
allegedly forged signatures,

496
00:31:42,701 --> 00:31:45,034
my experts will
counter your experts,

497
00:31:45,470 --> 00:31:47,598
my lawyers will
tie up your lawyers,

498
00:31:47,939 --> 00:31:51,341
and this project will be completed
and operating in the black

499
00:31:51,510 --> 00:31:53,775
long before this
matter can come to trial.

500
00:31:55,347 --> 00:31:57,714
You've wasted enough
of my time, Mr. Lewis.

501
00:31:57,883 --> 00:31:59,715
So, please,

502
00:32:00,485 --> 00:32:02,477
get off my land.

503
00:32:33,852 --> 00:32:36,014
Wait for me, please.

504
00:32:46,832 --> 00:32:47,856
- Hi.
- Hi.

505
00:32:48,033 --> 00:32:49,262
- Marilyn Reese.
- Jerri Stone.

506
00:32:49,434 --> 00:32:52,700
- I'm sorry, I don't have an appointment.
- Oh, don't worry about that. Sit down.

507
00:32:52,871 --> 00:32:56,103
You'd be surprised how
much walk-in business we get.

508
00:32:56,374 --> 00:32:59,902
Well, actually, I don't
need an attorney.

509
00:33:00,078 --> 00:33:03,014
Fact is, I work for
one. Ben Matlock.

510
00:33:03,782 --> 00:33:05,045
He's representing Lisa Swift.

511
00:33:05,217 --> 00:33:08,085
Do you remember her from
your Dixon and Parmelee days?

512
00:33:08,253 --> 00:33:09,277
Of course.

513
00:33:09,855 --> 00:33:12,188
She's been accused
of murdering Bruce.

514
00:33:14,326 --> 00:33:16,818
- So I heard.
- Oh.

515
00:33:17,796 --> 00:33:21,255
I understand that he
made a sizable deposit

516
00:33:21,433 --> 00:33:23,868
into your bank account right
after you moved here last year.

517
00:33:24,035 --> 00:33:25,435
That's correct.

518
00:33:26,104 --> 00:33:27,128
Why'd he do that?

519
00:33:27,806 --> 00:33:29,206
Severance pay.

520
00:33:30,609 --> 00:33:31,702
I thought you quit.

521
00:33:35,580 --> 00:33:36,741
What are you getting at?

522
00:33:37,916 --> 00:33:40,385
Sexual harassment.

523
00:33:48,627 --> 00:33:50,619
What I have to say
won't help Lisa a bit.

524
00:33:51,329 --> 00:33:53,821
You do realize that, don't you?

525
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:56,730
So tell me anyway.

526
00:34:08,980 --> 00:34:13,315
For about a year after I started
with Dixon and Parmelee,

527
00:34:13,485 --> 00:34:14,612
everything was fine.

528
00:34:15,120 --> 00:34:19,353
Bruce was wonderful.
Always the perfect gentleman.

529
00:34:20,392 --> 00:34:23,954
But all of a sudden, he changed.

530
00:34:24,729 --> 00:34:27,756
He started touching me
and propositioning me

531
00:34:27,933 --> 00:34:33,736
in this very subtle,
very insidious way.

532
00:34:35,874 --> 00:34:39,072
It quickly reached the
point where he made it clear,

533
00:34:39,244 --> 00:34:42,840
either I sleep with
him or lose my job.

534
00:34:43,014 --> 00:34:46,348
I didn't have a lot of
confidence in myself back then.

535
00:34:47,052 --> 00:34:50,250
And I figured he
would lose interest

536
00:34:50,422 --> 00:34:55,053
once he proved to me what a
big, strong, powerful man he was.

537
00:34:57,262 --> 00:34:59,060
But he didn't stop?

538
00:35:01,299 --> 00:35:02,927
No.

539
00:35:04,169 --> 00:35:05,967
No.

540
00:35:06,571 --> 00:35:10,167
It went on and on.

541
00:35:11,810 --> 00:35:13,506
Not that it didn't
have its perks.

542
00:35:14,212 --> 00:35:17,876
I made partner in record time.

543
00:35:19,050 --> 00:35:24,387
And he gave me a silver
picture frame, a Cartier watch,

544
00:35:28,994 --> 00:35:32,328
this lovely crystal doggie.

545
00:35:34,399 --> 00:35:35,639
But the more things he gave me,

546
00:35:35,767 --> 00:35:39,067
the harder it got for me to
look at myself in the mirror.

547
00:35:41,306 --> 00:35:45,767
So, finally, on the
day he gave me this,

548
00:35:46,878 --> 00:35:51,179
I hid a camera in his
bookcase and videotaped him

549
00:35:51,750 --> 00:35:53,378
in action.

550
00:35:56,454 --> 00:35:58,389
And then you blackmailed him.

551
00:36:00,492 --> 00:36:04,088
Quit my job, moved
here, passed the bar,

552
00:36:04,696 --> 00:36:07,632
and took the money he
paid me to start my own firm.

553
00:36:09,501 --> 00:36:11,970
I wasn't the only one he
was harassing, you know?

554
00:36:12,437 --> 00:36:15,703
At least, I'm
pretty sure I wasn't.

555
00:36:20,912 --> 00:36:22,540
How do you know that?

556
00:36:28,787 --> 00:36:30,119
A little doggie told me.

557
00:36:33,725 --> 00:36:35,660
Of course I remember
where I got it.

558
00:36:35,827 --> 00:36:37,352
Bruce gave it to me.

559
00:36:37,529 --> 00:36:40,693
Oh, well, why did
he give it to you?

560
00:36:40,865 --> 00:36:43,232
I suppose because he liked me.

561
00:36:43,401 --> 00:36:44,960
Did he give you this
picture frame too?

562
00:36:45,136 --> 00:36:46,900
Yes.

563
00:36:47,706 --> 00:36:50,107
I just had a long talk
with Marilyn Reese.

564
00:36:51,576 --> 00:36:58,278
Bruce gave Marilyn things too,
because she did certain favors.

565
00:36:59,150 --> 00:37:00,675
What's going on?

566
00:37:00,852 --> 00:37:03,287
Michael, this is Ben Matlock.

567
00:37:03,455 --> 00:37:05,788
I know who he is, Mom.

568
00:37:06,291 --> 00:37:08,556
- This about Mr. Parmelee?
- It doesn't concern you.

569
00:37:08,727 --> 00:37:10,007
She told me what a creep he was.

570
00:37:10,161 --> 00:37:13,563
- Michael, go to your room.
- Mom, stop treating me like a kid.

571
00:37:15,033 --> 00:37:16,763
You think I don't know
why he gave you this?

572
00:37:30,882 --> 00:37:33,613
I honestly thought
he didn't know.

573
00:37:36,921 --> 00:37:39,220
So, what Marilyn said is true?

574
00:37:42,293 --> 00:37:45,422
Bruce did to me what
he had done to her,

575
00:37:46,064 --> 00:37:49,660
only I didn't have the guts
to put an end to it like she did.

576
00:37:50,535 --> 00:37:54,404
I thought that I was home
free when he started in on Lisa,

577
00:37:54,572 --> 00:37:56,200
but then

578
00:37:57,008 --> 00:37:58,567
she blew it for me.

579
00:37:59,043 --> 00:38:01,877
- What did she do?
- She rejected him.

580
00:38:02,046 --> 00:38:07,781
The next thing I knew, he
was making me work late

581
00:38:08,453 --> 00:38:10,479
so he could start
up with me again.

582
00:38:11,289 --> 00:38:15,021
I think your son was
right. The guy was a creep.

583
00:38:15,927 --> 00:38:19,591
He started up with you
again the day of the murder?

584
00:38:20,532 --> 00:38:21,659
Yes.

585
00:38:23,368 --> 00:38:26,270
Well, where did you
go after he, um...?

586
00:38:26,604 --> 00:38:28,266
Home.

587
00:38:28,439 --> 00:38:31,841
I was here from 8:30
on. You can ask Michael.

588
00:38:33,278 --> 00:38:35,372
Oh, please,

589
00:38:35,747 --> 00:38:38,307
don't tell anyone about this.

590
00:38:38,716 --> 00:38:41,083
For Michael's sake.

591
00:38:41,619 --> 00:38:46,580
Life is tough enough
on him already.

592
00:38:47,292 --> 00:38:49,693
Please don't tell anyone.

593
00:39:03,741 --> 00:39:05,175
Okay.

594
00:39:05,343 --> 00:39:08,336
A minute, 28 seconds
for the gates to open,

595
00:39:08,513 --> 00:39:11,449
drive up, park, and
walk into the house.

596
00:39:11,616 --> 00:39:15,383
So if Bruce was alive when
he let Lisa through the gate,

597
00:39:15,553 --> 00:39:18,648
and dead when she came in
here a minute and 28 seconds later,

598
00:39:18,823 --> 00:39:20,018
what would you say?

599
00:39:20,191 --> 00:39:23,684
I guess the killer was in the
house when Bruce let her in.

600
00:39:23,862 --> 00:39:25,160
- That'd be my guess.
- Mine too.

601
00:39:25,330 --> 00:39:26,662
Mine too.

602
00:39:26,831 --> 00:39:29,960
Where do you suppose the
killer went after he stabbed him?

603
00:39:30,134 --> 00:39:32,899
The kitchen door
is locked with a key.

604
00:39:33,071 --> 00:39:34,733
Uh... A window.

605
00:39:34,906 --> 00:39:37,967
They were all closed
and locked from inside.

606
00:39:38,476 --> 00:39:42,436
Uh, maybe the
killer hid in a closet.

607
00:39:42,614 --> 00:39:45,607
- There's one in the hall.
- In the hall. Okay.

608
00:39:47,986 --> 00:39:54,415
Lisa said that his wife called the
police from a phone near the hall,

609
00:39:54,592 --> 00:39:58,620
and stayed there
till they got here.

610
00:40:00,031 --> 00:40:01,499
Go upstairs?

611
00:40:01,666 --> 00:40:04,659
Yes. Try that.

612
00:40:13,211 --> 00:40:16,010
- All right, you wanna start at this end?
- Yeah.

613
00:40:20,318 --> 00:40:24,255
- Boy's room.
- Yeah, I think he's off at college.

614
00:40:24,722 --> 00:40:26,418
Look at this.

615
00:40:30,161 --> 00:40:32,824
- This window could've been opened.
- Mm-hm.

616
00:40:32,997 --> 00:40:35,967
Hey, we used to have a tree like that
outside my window when I was a kid.

617
00:40:36,134 --> 00:40:39,229
Came in real handy when I
wanted to sneak out at night.

618
00:40:39,570 --> 00:40:43,371
I don't know. That'd be quite
a jump to the closest limb.

619
00:40:43,541 --> 00:40:45,669
No, that wouldn't
be any problem.

620
00:40:45,843 --> 00:40:47,106
Hey, look. See?

621
00:40:47,445 --> 00:40:49,505
Someone carved something
in that branch up there.

622
00:40:49,681 --> 00:40:51,411
Probably his kid.

623
00:41:03,261 --> 00:41:06,959
Michael, you love your mother?

624
00:41:08,566 --> 00:41:09,590
Yeah.

625
00:41:10,635 --> 00:41:13,469
- Do you get along?
- Yeah.

626
00:41:14,272 --> 00:41:15,934
I mean, most of the time.

627
00:41:16,107 --> 00:41:18,838
We argue and stuff, but that
doesn't mean I don't love her.

628
00:41:19,644 --> 00:41:21,442
Where's your father?

629
00:41:21,612 --> 00:41:24,275
I haven't seen him in ten years.

630
00:41:25,683 --> 00:41:29,017
He dropped me off at school
one day, and that was it.

631
00:41:29,253 --> 00:41:30,585
Hm.

632
00:41:32,056 --> 00:41:35,549
How did you feel
about Bruce Parmelee?

633
00:41:35,727 --> 00:41:38,060
- He was a creep.
- What makes you say that?

634
00:41:38,463 --> 00:41:39,658
He just was.

635
00:41:40,398 --> 00:41:42,026
Did your mother tell
you he was a creep?

636
00:41:42,367 --> 00:41:43,995
No.

637
00:41:44,235 --> 00:41:48,366
He made her work late
long after the others had left

638
00:41:48,539 --> 00:41:50,735
at least once a week, didn't he?

639
00:41:51,309 --> 00:41:52,709
Yeah.

640
00:41:52,877 --> 00:41:55,312
Did you think there was
something going on between them?

641
00:41:55,480 --> 00:41:59,975
I mean, I'm not saying there was,
but that is what you thought, isn't it?

642
00:42:01,185 --> 00:42:02,881
Yeah, I guess.

643
00:42:03,054 --> 00:42:05,922
Did you think they
were having an affair?

644
00:42:08,326 --> 00:42:13,094
I thought he was, you
know, making her do stuff

645
00:42:13,264 --> 00:42:14,732
just because he was her boss.

646
00:42:14,899 --> 00:42:16,993
Well, what made you think that?

647
00:42:18,536 --> 00:42:22,234
She'd come home from work
real late, upset, you know.

648
00:42:22,407 --> 00:42:26,708
Like she was embarrassed
or ashamed or something.

649
00:42:27,245 --> 00:42:30,374
And how did that make you feel?

650
00:42:30,648 --> 00:42:33,618
Useless? Or angry? Or both?

651
00:42:33,785 --> 00:42:37,517
I don't know. I
don't know how I felt.

652
00:42:42,894 --> 00:42:47,798
Your mother had to work late

653
00:42:49,200 --> 00:42:51,635
the night Bruce
Parmelee died, didn't she?

654
00:42:51,803 --> 00:42:52,827
Yeah.

655
00:42:53,004 --> 00:42:55,303
Was she upset when
she came home that night?

656
00:42:56,174 --> 00:42:57,198
Yeah.

657
00:42:57,375 --> 00:42:58,399
How could you tell?

658
00:42:59,877 --> 00:43:02,039
I heard her crying while
she was in the bathroom.

659
00:43:03,147 --> 00:43:04,547
What did you do?

660
00:43:06,918 --> 00:43:08,284
Nothing?

661
00:43:11,522 --> 00:43:16,051
Your mother tells me that
you watch a lot of television.

662
00:43:16,227 --> 00:43:17,786
Is that what you
were doing that night?

663
00:43:17,962 --> 00:43:19,225
Yeah.

664
00:43:19,397 --> 00:43:21,992
There was a big
mini-series on that week.

665
00:43:22,166 --> 00:43:23,725
- Is that what you watched?
- Yeah.

666
00:43:24,001 --> 00:43:26,095
Definitely. They had that, um...

667
00:43:26,270 --> 00:43:28,637
They took it from the
best-selling horror book.

668
00:43:28,806 --> 00:43:32,800
Yeah, that's it. Only it
wasn't on, on Thursday night.

669
00:43:32,977 --> 00:43:35,139
So you didn't watch
television that night.

670
00:43:36,180 --> 00:43:37,876
Unless I missed my guess,

671
00:43:38,049 --> 00:43:39,779
you'd gone over to
Bruce Parmelee's house,

672
00:43:39,951 --> 00:43:41,317
- hadn't you?
- No.

673
00:43:41,486 --> 00:43:43,955
Since your car wasn't parked
out front when Lisa got there,

674
00:43:44,122 --> 00:43:46,148
I imagine you
parked on the street,

675
00:43:46,324 --> 00:43:48,987
then climbed over the fence
and found your way into the house.

676
00:43:49,160 --> 00:43:52,597
Look, the reason why my car
wasn't there is because I wasn't there.

677
00:43:52,763 --> 00:43:54,755
I know you were there, Michael.

678
00:43:54,932 --> 00:43:56,491
You're a good, honest boy

679
00:43:56,667 --> 00:43:59,102
who couldn't allow your
mother to be victimized anymore.

680
00:43:59,270 --> 00:44:01,330
Bruce Parmelee was a bully.

681
00:44:01,506 --> 00:44:04,533
You knew what he was doing,
and it ate at you and ate at you

682
00:44:04,709 --> 00:44:06,469
until finally you couldn't
handle it anymore.

683
00:44:06,611 --> 00:44:08,136
Isn't that the way it happened?

684
00:44:08,312 --> 00:44:11,180
Isn't it true that you
couldn't live with it anymore?

685
00:44:11,349 --> 00:44:13,069
Didn't you go over to
Bruce Parmelee's house

686
00:44:13,217 --> 00:44:14,617
- to protect your mother?
- Yeah.

687
00:44:15,486 --> 00:44:18,422
Yeah, I told him to keep
his dirty hands off her.

688
00:44:18,589 --> 00:44:21,024
He was a creep and
somebody had to make him stop.

689
00:44:22,593 --> 00:44:24,152
Did you get mad?

690
00:44:25,897 --> 00:44:28,457
When Miss Swift showed up,

691
00:44:28,633 --> 00:44:31,626
he told me to leave
or he'd call the police.

692
00:44:33,404 --> 00:44:36,670
And when I wouldn't
go, he pushed me.

693
00:44:37,909 --> 00:44:41,243
And you lost your temper
altogether, didn't you?

694
00:44:41,412 --> 00:44:43,210
- No.
- And you grabbed

695
00:44:43,381 --> 00:44:45,441
a letter opener off the desk.

696
00:44:45,616 --> 00:44:48,085
And before you knew what
you were doing, you stabbed him.

697
00:44:48,252 --> 00:44:49,811
- No.
- When you saw what you'd done,

698
00:44:49,987 --> 00:44:53,424
you realized Lisa was coming up,
ran up the stairs, opened a window

699
00:44:53,591 --> 00:44:56,220
and made your escape
down the tree outside.

700
00:44:56,394 --> 00:44:57,828
No.

701
00:44:58,029 --> 00:44:59,998
Son,

702
00:45:00,932 --> 00:45:05,131
these blue threads were
found by police technicians

703
00:45:05,303 --> 00:45:09,070
in the tree outside the
Parmelee house yesterday.

704
00:45:10,174 --> 00:45:14,077
And this morning,
with a warrant,

705
00:45:14,845 --> 00:45:17,440
they found this blue
sweater in your closet.

706
00:45:19,517 --> 00:45:21,850
You know they match.

707
00:45:24,355 --> 00:45:27,723
Michael, you're
barely 17 years old.

708
00:45:28,993 --> 00:45:33,829
You were trying to help your mother, I
guess, in the only way you knew how.

709
00:45:33,998 --> 00:45:35,864
I'm sorry.

710
00:45:36,767 --> 00:45:43,105
I'm very sorry, but I can't allow
someone else to take the blame.

711
00:45:43,674 --> 00:45:45,802
I hope someday
you'll understand.

712
00:46:01,158 --> 00:46:03,821
All right, what do you
mean you solved the case?

713
00:46:03,995 --> 00:46:06,362
I solved the case. Ben,
didn't I solve the case?

714
00:46:06,530 --> 00:46:09,398
- Could not have done it without him.
- Thank you.

715
00:46:09,567 --> 00:46:11,263
So should we go
out and celebrate?

716
00:46:11,435 --> 00:46:12,528
You all go.

717
00:46:12,703 --> 00:46:16,196
I think I'd like to spend a
nice quiet evening by myself.

718
00:46:16,374 --> 00:46:18,240
Ben?

719
00:46:18,542 --> 00:46:20,477
Ben, is that you? Oh.

720
00:46:20,645 --> 00:46:22,477
It's about time you got home.

721
00:46:22,647 --> 00:46:24,946
Here. While I was waiting
for you, I fixed your remote.

722
00:46:25,116 --> 00:46:26,812
Oh, well, good.

723
00:46:26,984 --> 00:46:29,476
Got good news about
that house next door.

724
00:46:29,654 --> 00:46:31,088
You didn't raise your offer?

725
00:46:31,255 --> 00:46:33,349
No, no. I had it inspected.

726
00:46:33,524 --> 00:46:35,652
You were right.
It's a disaster area.

727
00:46:36,327 --> 00:46:39,957
Termites, dry rot,
asbestos ceilings,

728
00:46:40,131 --> 00:46:44,660
lead paint, radon
gas, oil fumes.

729
00:46:44,835 --> 00:46:47,498
This Waldo Finney
guy can have it.

730
00:46:47,672 --> 00:46:49,800
He'll never be able to
live in it, much less sell it,

731
00:46:49,974 --> 00:46:53,035
unless he tears it all
down and starts all over.

732
00:46:53,611 --> 00:46:57,548
Yeah, I figure he must
be really, really rich,

733
00:46:57,715 --> 00:46:59,115
or really, really stupid.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
